{
"opening": "This release introduces several new functions and improves existing ones to enhance the library's functionality and performance. It also updates compatibility to support Python 3.10 and removes legacy Python 2 support.",
"requirements": [
{
"id": "m02",
"text": "The public API must expose a function named compose_left that accepts a function and arguments, returning a new callable that applies the function from left to right."
},
{
"id": "m03",
"text": "The public API must expose a function named peekn that takes an iterable and a count, yielding items from the iterable while allowing inspection of the first n items without consuming them entirely in a single pass if not needed."
},
{
"id": "m04",
"text": "The dissoc function must perform its operation on dictionaries with improved speed compared to previous versions, maintaining the same input signature and output format."
},
{
"id": "m05",
"text": "The Compose class and compose function must provide enhanced capabilities for composing multiple functions together, ensuring correct argument handling and return values as per standard composition semantics."
},
{
"id": "m07",
"text": "The fold function must utilize multiprocessing to parallelize the reduction operation across iterables, improving performance for large datasets while maintaining the same interface and result structure."
},
{
"id": "m14",
"text": "The merge and merge_with functions must accept any Mapping type, not just dict instances, for their inputs, while preserving the existing behavior and output format for merging mappings."
},
{
"id": "m18",
"text": "All listed public names must operate exclusively under Python 3 syntax and semantics, removing any conditional logic or fallbacks specific to Python 2."
},
{
"id": "m28",
"text": "The library must correctly handle the anext built-in function signature in Python 3.10 environments, ensuring no import errors or type mismatches occur when using related functional tools."
}
],
"closing": "Please ensure all changes are tested against the supported Python versions and that the public API remains stable and well-documented."
}
